    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d149">

                <pb facs="0166" n="154" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg154"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00/1100: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Oslo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-04-24T12:00:00+01:00">April 24,
                            1939—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received April 24—8:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>19. My 18, April 22, 1 p.m.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d149-f1"/> Foreign Office
                    informs me that the Foreign Minister replied orally in the negative to the
                    German Minister’s questions whether Norway felt itself threatened by Germany and
                    whether the Norwegian Government had “authorized” or in other way had occasioned
                    the President’s message but that the Foreign Minister had added that obviously
                    Norwegians, Norway knew, would be in danger if there were a general war and that
                    therefore Norway was putting its neutrality guard in readiness.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
